\name{proteins_update_go}
\alias{proteins_update_go}
\title{Download the current Gene Ontology release to a go.obo, on purpose}
\usage{
proteins_update_go(go_obo, timeout = NULL)
}
\arguments{
\item{go_obo}{Where to write, e.g. \code{"go.obo"}. Its folder must exist.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} (the default) to wait; mzLib itself gives up after two
  minutes without data.
}

}
\description{
Download the current Gene Ontology release (go.obo) to a path, on purpose, keeping any different file already there as a timestamped backup.

Wraps mzLib's \code{Loaders.UpdateGeneOntology}. The whole go.obo (tens of megabytes) is streamed from
GO's PURL, which always serves the \strong{current} release. When a file is already at the path, it is
kept beside the new one as \code{go.obo.<yyyyMMdd-HHmmss-fff>} if the download differs, and left alone
if it is the same, so earlier runs stay reproducible. A failed download leaves any existing file
untouched.
}
\details{
This is the only function in mzLibR that fetches a go.obo. \code{\link{proteins_annotate_go}} never does,
so the release a result was computed against is always a file you chose to keep.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_go_update}: \code{go_obo_file} written; \code{url} fetched from; \code{existed_before};
  \code{previous_sha256} of the file that was there, \code{NA} when there was none; \code{changed}, whether the
  file on disk is now different; \code{go}, the release now at the path (\code{source_file_name},
  \code{sha256}, \code{release}, \code{term_count}); and \code{caveats}, which name the backup kept.

}
